The Code aligns with European Union environmental directives and international agreements, ensuring Swedish environmental governance meets global standards. It promotes transparency and accountability, requiring comprehensive reporting and adherence to strict environmental standards.', 'Sweden', 'climate', 'in_force', '1998-01-01'::date, 'Sweden', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/171_14', ARRAY['sdg13', 'regulations', 'sdg7', 'mandatory', 'sdg6', 'sdg16', 'sdg12', 'sweden', 'sdg15']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('1a6055df-c460-54f9-b16b-2266f7a89eb2', 'Sweden Swedish Annual Accounts Act', 'Swedish Annual Accounts Act', 'The Swedish Annual Accounts Act (1995:1554), enacted on December 14, 1995, and amended through SFS 2024:1115, regulates financial reporting for companies operating in Sweden. The law requires annual accounts to present a fair and transparent view of a company’s financial position and results, aligning with EU directives on corporate reporting. The act applies to limited liability companies, economic associations, and other entities specified under Swedish law, with additional provisions for public-interest entities such as listed companies.

The act mandates the inclusion of non-financial disclosures on sustainability, particularly for large companies with over 250 employees. These disclosures cover environmental, social, and governance (ESG) matters, including climate risks, diversity policies, and anti-corruption measures. It also requires companies to provide a management report detailing financial performance, significant risks, and future prospects.

Key objectives include enhancing transparency, fostering investor confidence, and aligning Swedish corporate practices with international standards. The law encourages sustainable development by emphasizing ESG criteria, supporting Sweden’s environmental and social goals.', 'The Swedish Annual Accounts Act (1995:1554), enacted on December 14, 1995, and amended through SFS 2024:1115, regulates financial reporting for companies operating in Sweden. The law requires annual accounts to present a fair and transparent view of a company’s financial position and results, aligning with EU directives on corporate reporting. The act applies to limited liability companies, economic associations, and other entities specified under Swedish law, with additional provisions for public-interest entities such as listed companies.

The act mandates the inclusion of non-financial disclosures on sustainability, particularly for large companies with over 250 employees. These disclosures cover environmental, social, and governance (ESG) matters, including climate risks, diversity policies, and anti-corruption measures. It also requires companies to provide a management report detailing financial performance, significant risks, and future prospects.

Key objectives include enhancing transparency, fostering investor confidence, and aligning Swedish corporate practices with international standards. The law encourages sustainable development by emphasizing ESG criteria, supporting Sweden’s environmental and social goals.', 'The Swedish Annual Accounts Act (1995:1554), enacted on December 14, 1995, and amended through SFS 2024:1115, regulates financial reporting for companies operating in Sweden. The law requires annual accounts to present a fair and transparent view of a company’s financial position and results, aligning with EU directives on corporate reporting. The act applies to limited liability companies, economic associations, and other entities specified under Swedish law, with additional provisions for public-interest entities such as listed companies.

